   "evidence": "Grep over ./upstream/mathlib finds no `ofFn`/`reverse` lemma (`reverse.*ofFn|ofFn.*reverse`: no matches; `ofFn_rev|ofFn_reverse|reverse_ofFn`: no matches), so the list identity is the genuinely new content; `prod_inv_reverse` and `map_ofFn` are both present upstream (Mathlib/Data/List/OfFn.lean:40,98).",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Mathematics/Algebra/BigOperators/Fin.lean",
   "fix": "State the reversal lemma for an arbitrary type (`lemma List.ofFn_rev {\u03b1} {n} (f : Fin n \u2192 \u03b1) : List.ofFn (fun i => f i.rev) = (List.ofFn f).reverse`), and obtain the group statement as a one-liner from it plus `List.map_ofFn` and `List.prod_inv_reverse` (keeping `prod_ofFn_inv_rev` as that corollary is fine).",
   "issue": "`List.prod_ofFn_inv_rev` bundles a `Group` assumption with content that needs no algebra: the new fact is `List.ofFn (fun i => f i.rev) = (List.ofFn f).reverse`, true for any type, while the inversion step is Mathlib's `List.prod_inv_reverse` (upstream/mathlib/Mathlib/Algebra/BigOperators/Group/List/Basic.lean:312, `L.prod\u207b\u00b9 = (L.map fun x => x\u207b\u00b9).reverse.prod`), which the induction at lines 57-60 re-proves for the `ofFn` special case. In a file explicitly mirroring a Mathlib path for upstreaming, the reusable lemma is locked behind `[Group G]`.",
   "line": 54
  }
 ],
